# Direction of picture and sound

## Picture
- **Mood**: quiet, tender, a little wistful; a bedtime-story feeling.
- **Palette**: cool dusk blues and violets for sky, water and village, set against the single warm amber/orange of the lantern (and the warm village windows). The lantern is always the warmest, most saturated thing in frame, so the eye follows it to the last shot.
- **Style**: one illustrated style for every shot (e.g. soft painterly / storybook illustration); the production-designer chooses and holds it. No photoreal-to-cartoon jumps between shots, no change of rendering technique.
- **Consistency to hold**: the same child (age, hair, clothes, colours) in shots 2 and 3; the same lantern (shape, paper colour, frame, glow) in shots 2–5; the same village in shots 1 and 4.
- **Text**: title and end card in one clean, legible typeface, light on dark, inside the title-safe area; no other on-screen text, no watermarks.

## Sound
- **Ambience** for the whole film: gentle water (lapping at the bank, a soft river flow) and evening (crickets or frogs, perhaps a distant bird settling), quiet and continuous across cuts.
- **Music cue**: one soft cue (e.g. solo piano, music box, harp or flute, slow and simple), entering under the title, swelling gently at the release (shot 3), resolving in shot 5, its last note decaying over the end card.
- **No dialogue, no voice, no lyrics.**
- **Mix**: music sits above the ambience but never buries it; no clicks at cuts, no clipping; fade in from silence at 00:00 and out to silence by the end.
